WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.600
An agent attack surface includes every place where instructions, data, identity, state, or actions enter or leave the system.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:24.500
Prompts, memory stores, uploaded files, retrieved documents, external websites, user inputs, tools, APIs, plugins, model providers, logs, and connected applications can all affect the agent's behavior or expose information.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.840
Each connection should have a documented purpose and owner.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.860
Trust boundaries are especially important because agents mix content from different sources.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:39.480
A system instruction may be trusted configuration, while a document or tool response may be untrusted data.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.980
The instruction path asks who can influence the task.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.600
The data path asks what can be read, stored, or disclosed.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.940
The action path asks what systems can be changed.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.680
Risk increases when these paths connect without strong controls.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.020
Identity and credentials are also exposure points.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.880
Teams should know whether each tool call runs as the current user, a shared service identity, or a privileged application account.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:14.780
Tokens, secrets, delegated access, and service-to-service permissions need limited scope, protected storage, rotation, and revocation.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.120
Third-party connectors and libraries should be included in dependency and change-management processes.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:28.500
A useful attack-surface review produces a data-flow and trust-boundary diagram, an inventory of tools and data sources, and a record of assumptions.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.880
For each component, document what it receives, what it trusts, what it can access, and what it can change.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:43.580
That evidence helps teams place authorization, validation, isolation, approvals, rate limits, logging, and monitoring where they reduce meaningful risk.
